\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 11
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Externalities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
11.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this chapter we deal with the case of externalities so that a market
 equilibrium may lead to non-Pareto efficient allocations in general, and
 thus there is a market failure.
 The reason is that there are things that people care about that are not
 priced.
 An externality can happen in both cases of consumption and production.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Consumption Externality:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 : without preference externality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})$
\end_inset

 : with preference externality, in which other individuals' consumptions
 effect an individual's utility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
11.1.1.

\series default
 (i) One person's quiet environment is disturbed by another person's local
 stereo.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
(ii) Mr.
 A hates Mr.
 B smoking next to him
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
(iii) Mr.
 A's satisfaction decreases as Mr.
 C's consumption level increases because Mr.
 A envies Mr.
 C's lifestyle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Production Externality:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A firm's production includes arguments other than its own inputs.
 For example, downstream fishing is adversely affected by pollutants emitted
 from an upstream chemical plant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This leads to an examination of various suggestions for alternative ways
 to allocate resources that may lead to efficient outcomes.
 Achieving an efficient allocation in the presence of externalities essentially
 involves making sure that agents face the correct prices for their actions.
 Ways of solving an externality problem included taxation, regulation, property
 rights, merges, etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
11.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consumption Externalities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When there are no consumption externalities, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's utility function is a function of only his own consumption:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u_{i}(x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In this case, the first order conditions for the competitive equilibrium
 are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MRS_{xy}^{A}=\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}=MRS_{xy}^{B}
\]

\end_inset

and the first-order conditions for Pareto efficiency are given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MRS_{xy}^{A}=MRS_{xy}^{B}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, because of price-taking behavior, every competitive equilibrium implies
 Pareto efficiency if utility functions are quasi-concave.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The main purpose of this section is to show that a competitive equilibrium
 allocation is not in general Pareto efficient when there exists an externality
 in consumption.
 We show this by examining that the first order conditions for a competitive
 equilibrium are not in general the same as the first-order conditions for
 Pareto efficient allocations in the presence of consumptions externalities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the following simple two-person and two-good exchange economy.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
u_{A}(x_{A},x_{B},y_{A})\\
u_{B}(x_{A},x_{B},y_{B})
\end{gather}

\end_inset

which are assumed to be strictly increasing in his own goods consumption,
 quasi-concave, and satisfies the Inada condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{x_{i}\rightarrow0}\frac{\partial u}{\partial x_{i}}(x_{i})=+\infty$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{x_{i}\rightarrow+\infty}\frac{\partial u}{\partial x_{i}}(x_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 so it results in interior solutions.
 We further assume that the gradient of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is nonzero at Pareto efficient allocations.
 Here, good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 results in consumption externalities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first-order conditions for the competitive equilibrium are the same
 as before:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MRS_{xy}^{A}=\frac{p_{x}}{p_{y}}=MRS_{xy}^{B}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now find the first order conditions for Pareto efficient allocations
 in exchange economies with externalities.
 Thus, Pareto efficient allocations 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 can be completely determined by the FOCs of the following problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{x\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{4}}} & u_{B}(x_{A},x_{B},y_{B})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{A}+x_{B}\leq w_{x}\\
 & y_{A}+y_{B}\leq w_{y}\\
 & u_{A}(x_{A},x_{B},y_{A})\geq u_{A}(x_{A}^{*},x_{B}^{*},y_{A}^{*})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The first-order conditions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
x_{A} & : & \frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}-\lambda_{x}+\mu\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}=0\\
y_{A} & : & -\lambda_{y}+\mu\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}=0\\
x_{B} & : & \frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}-\lambda_{x}+\mu\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}=0\\
y_{B} & : & \frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}-\lambda_{y}=0\\
\lambda_{x} & : & w_{x}-x_{A}-x_{B}\geq0,\ \lambda_{x}\geq0,\ \lambda_{x}(w_{x}-x_{A}-x_{B})=0\\
\lambda_{y} & : & w_{y}-y_{A}-y_{B}\geq0,\ \lambda_{y}\geq0,\ \lambda_{y}(w_{y}-y_{A}-y_{B})=0\\
\mu & : & u_{A}-u_{A}^{*}\geq0,\ \mu\geq0,\ \mu(u_{A}-u_{A}^{*})=0
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

By (7), 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{y}=\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}>0$
\end_inset

, and thus, by (9)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which means there is never destruction of the good which does not exhibit
 a negative externality.
 Also, by (5) and (7), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mu=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Then, by (4) and (5), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\lambda_{x}}{\lambda_{y}}=\left[\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and by (6) and (7), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\lambda_{x}}{\lambda_{y}}=\left[\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, by (13) and (14), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}\geq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(SMRS_{A}=SMRS_{B})$
\end_inset

 which expreses the equality of the social marginal rates of substitution
 for the two consumers at Pareto efficient points, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
SMRS_{A}=SMRS_{B}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, we immediately have the following conclusion: A competitive equilibrium
 allocation may not be Pareto optimal because the first-order conditions
 for competitive equilibrium and Pareto optimality are not the same.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the above marginal equality condition, we know that, in order to evaluate
 the relevant marginal rates of substitution for the optimality conditions,
 we must take into account both the direct and indirect effects of consumption
 activities in the presence of externalities.
 That is, to achieve Pareto optimality, when one consumer increases the
 consumption of good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, not only does the consumer's consumption of good 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 need to change, the other consumer's consumption of good 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 must also be changed.
 Therefore, the social marginal rate of substitution of good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 for good 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 by consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 equals
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}}{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial y_{i}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{j}}{\partial x_{i}}}{\frac{\partial u_{j}}{\partial y_{j}}}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Solving (4) and (6) for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{x}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mu=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}-\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial x_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}-\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}>0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda_{x}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}-\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}-\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When the consumption externality is positive, from (13) or (14), we can
 easily see that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{x}$
\end_inset

 is always positive since
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\lambda_{y}=\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}>0
\]

\end_inset

Also, when no externality or a one-sided externality exists
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Only one consumer imposes an externality on another consumer.
\end_layout

\end_inset

, by either (13) or (14), 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{x}$
\end_inset

 is positive.
 Thus, the marginal equality condition (15) and the balanced conditions
 completely determine all Pareto efficient allocations for these cases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, when there are negative externalities for both consumers, the Kuhn-Tuck
er multiplier 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{x}$
\end_inset

 directly given by (17), or indirectly by (13) or (14), is the sum of a
 negative and positive term, and thus the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{x}$
\end_inset

 may be indeterminate.
 However, unlike the claim in some textbooks, the marginal equality condition,
 (15), and the balanced conditions may not guarantee finding Pareto efficient
 allocations correctly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To guarantee that an allocation is Pareto efficient in the presence of negative
 externalities, we must require 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{x}\geq0$
\end_inset

 (required for optimality) at efficient points, which in turn requires that
 social marginal rates of substitution be nonnegative, that is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}\geq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or equivalently requires both (15) and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}\geq\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for all Pareto efficient points.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can interpret the term in the left-hand side of (19), 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}$
\end_inset

, as the joint marginal benefit of consuming good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, and the term in the right-hand side, 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}$
\end_inset

, as the joint marginal cost of consuming good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 because the negative externality hurts the consumers.
 To consume the goods efficiently, a necessary condition is that the joint
 marginal benefit of consuming good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 should not be less than the joint cost of consuming good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus the following conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(PO)\left\{ \begin{array}{c}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}\geq0\\
y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}\\
x_{A}+x_{B}\leq w_{x}\\
\left(\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}-\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}\right)\left(w_{x}-x_{A}-x_{B}\right)=0
\end{array}\right.
\]

\end_inset

constitute a system 
\begin_inset Formula $(PO)$
\end_inset

 from which all Pareto efficient allocations can be obtained.
 We can do so by considering three cases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Case 1.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}\geq\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}
\]

\end_inset

or equivalently
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}>0,\ \lambda_{x}>0
\]

\end_inset

and thus the last two conditions in the above system 
\begin_inset Formula $(PO)$
\end_inset

 reduce to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}+x_{B}=w_{x}$
\end_inset

.
 In this ase, there is no destruction.
 Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}+x_{B}=w_{x}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}$
\end_inset

 into the marginal equality condition (15), it would give us a relationship
 between 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}$
\end_inset

, which exactly defines the Pareto efficient allocaitons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Case 2.
 When the joint marginal benefit equals the joint marginal cost
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}=\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{x}=0$
\end_inset

.
 In this case, when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}+x_{B}\leq w_{x}$
\end_inset

, the necessity of destruction is indeterminant.
 However, even when destruction is necessary, we can still determine the
 set of Pareto efficient allocations by using 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}$
\end_inset

 and the zero social marginal equality conditions (21).
 Indeed, after substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}$
\end_inset

 into (21), we can solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}$
\end_inset

 in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Case 3.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}<\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}
\]

\end_inset

for any allocations that satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}+x_{B}=w_{x}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}$
\end_inset

, and the marginal equality condition (15), the social marginal rates of
 substitution must be negative.
 Hence, the allocation will not be Pareto efficient.
 In this case, there must be destruction for good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 for Pareto efficiency, and a Pareto efficient allocation satisfies (21).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Summarizing, we have the following proposition that provides two categories
 of sufficiency conditions for characterizing whether or not there should
 be destruciton of endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{x}$
\end_inset

 in achieving Pareto efficient allocations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
11.2.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
For 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times2$
\end_inset

 pure exchange economies, suppose that utility functions 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{A},x_{B},y_{i})$
\end_inset

 are continuously differentiable, strictly quasi-concave, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{i}(x_{A},x_{B},y_{i})}{\partial x_{i}}>0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=A,B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
If the social marginal rates of substitution are positive at a Pareto efficient
 allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
As we discussed previously, this is true if the consumpiton externality
 is positive, if there is no externality, or if there is only a one side
 externality.
\end_layout

\end_inset

, then there is no destruction of 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{x}$
\end_inset

 in achieving Pareto efficient allocaiton 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If the social marginal rates of substitution are negative for any allocation
 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{A},x_{B})$
\end_inset

 satisfying 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}+x_{B}=w_{x}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}$
\end_inset

, and the marginal equality condition (15), then there is destruciton of
 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{x}$
\end_inset

 in achieving any Pareto efficient allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 That is, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}^{*}+x_{B}^{*}<w_{x}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is determined by 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}$
\end_inset

 and (21).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, from the above proposition, we know that a sufficient condition for
 destruction is that for any allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{A},y_{A},x_{B},y_{B})$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left.\begin{array}{c}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}\\
x_{A}+x_{B}=w_{x}\\
y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}
\end{array}\right\} \implies\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}<\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

A sufficient condition for all Pareto efficient allocations 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{A},y_{A},x_{B},y_{B})$
\end_inset

 for no destruction is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left.\begin{array}{c}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}\\
x_{A}+x_{B}\leq w_{x}\\
y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}
\end{array}\right\} \implies\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}\geq\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
11.2.1.

\series default
 Consider the following function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}(x_{A},x_{B},y_{i})=\sqrt{x_{i}y_{i}}-x_{j},\ i\in\{A,B\},\ j\in\{A,B\},\ j\neq i
\]

\end_inset

By the marginal equality condition (15), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\frac{1}{2}(x_{A}y_{A})^{-\frac{1}{2}}y_{A}}{\frac{1}{2}(x_{A}y_{A})^{-\frac{1}{2}}x_{A}}+\frac{-1}{\frac{1}{2}(x_{B}y_{B})^{-\frac{1}{2}}x_{B}} & = & \frac{\frac{1}{2}(x_{B}y_{B})^{-\frac{1}{2}}y_{B}}{\frac{1}{2}(x_{B}y_{B})^{-\frac{1}{2}}x_{B}}+\frac{-1}{\frac{1}{2}(x_{A}y_{A})^{-\frac{1}{2}}x_{A}}\nonumber \\
\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}}-2\frac{(x_{B}y_{B})^{\frac{1}{2}}}{x_{B}} & = & \frac{y_{B}}{x_{B}}-2\frac{(x_{A}y_{A})^{\frac{1}{2}}}{x_{A}}\nonumber \\
\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}}+2\sqrt{\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}}}+1 & = & \frac{y_{B}}{x_{B}}+\sqrt{\frac{y_{B}}{x_{B}}}+1\nonumber \\
\left(\sqrt{\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}}}+1\right)^{2} & = & \left(\sqrt{\frac{y_{B}}{x_{B}}}+1\right)^{2}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

and thus
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}}=\frac{y_{B}}{x_{B}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}+x_{B}\equiv\bar{x}$
\end_inset

.
 Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}+x_{B}=\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}$
\end_inset

 into (25), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}} & = & \frac{y_{B}}{x_{B}}\nonumber \\
\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}} & = & \frac{(w_{y}-y_{A})}{(\bar{x}-x_{A})}\nonumber \\
y_{A}\bar{x}-y_{A}x_{A} & = & x_{A}w_{y}-y_{A}x_{A}\nonumber \\
y_{A}\bar{x}-x_{A}w_{y} & = & 0\nonumber \\
\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}}\bar{x}-w_{y} & = & 0\nonumber \\
\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}} & = & \frac{w_{y}}{\bar{x}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Then, by (25) and (26), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}=\frac{1}{4}\sqrt{\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}}}\sqrt{\frac{y_{B}}{x_{B}}}=\frac{y_{A}}{4x_{A}}=\frac{w_{y}}{4\bar{x}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}=1
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}=\frac{w_{y}}{4}$
\end_inset

 is the critical point that makes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}-\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}=0
\]

\end_inset

or equivalently
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}=0
\]

\end_inset

Hence, if 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{x}>\frac{w_{y}}{4}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}-\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}<0
\]

\end_inset

 and thus, by (22), there is destruction in any Pareto efficient allocation.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{x}<\frac{w_{y}}{4}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}-\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}>0
\]

\end_inset

and by (23), no Pareto optimal allocation requires destruction.
 Finally, when 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{x}=\frac{w_{y}}{4}$
\end_inset

, any allocation that satisfies the marginal equality condition (15) and
 the balanced conditions 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}+x_{B}=w_{x}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}$
\end_inset

 also satisfies (19), and thus it is a Pareto efficient allocation with
 no destruction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, let us set out the sufficient conditions for destruction and non-destrucito
n in detail.
 Note that if the sufficient condition for non-destruciton holds, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{A}+x_{B}=w_{x}$
\end_inset

 would also hold as an implication.
 Thus, to apply both the sufficient conditions for non-destruction and destrucit
on, one should use the following three conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\{ \begin{array}{c}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial y_{B}}}+\frac{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}}{\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial y_{A}}}\\
x_{A}+x_{B}=w_{x}\\
y_{A}+y_{B}=w_{y}
\end{array}\right.
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If any allocation also satisfies the condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}\geq\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}
\]

\end_inset

then the allocation is Pareto efficient, and further there is no destruciton.
 Otherwise, it is not Pareto efficient.
 If this is true for all such non-Pareto allocations, all Pareto efficient
 conditions must hold with destruction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that since 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{A}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{B}}$
\end_inset

 represent marginal benefit, they are usually diminishing in consumption
 in good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{A}}{\partial x_{B}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial u_{B}}{\partial x_{A}}$
\end_inset

 are in the form of a marginal cost, their absolute values would be typically
 increasing in the consumption of good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, when total endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{x}$
\end_inset

 is small, the social margianl benefit would exceed the social marginal
 cost, so that there is no destruction.
 As the total endowment of 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{x}$
\end_inset

 increases, the social marginal cost will ultimately outweigh the marginal
 social benefit, which results in the destruction of the endowment of 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{x}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Alternatively, we can get the same result by using social marginal rates
 of substitution.
 When utilty functions are strictly quasi-concave, marginal rates of substitutio
n are diminishing.
 Therefore, in the presence of negative consumption externalities, social
 marginal rates of substitution may become negative when the consumption
 of good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 becomes sufficiently large.
 When this occurs, it is better to destroy some resources for good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 As the destruction of good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 increases, which will, in turn, decrease the consumption of good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, social marginal rates of substitution will increase.
 Eventually, they will become nonnegative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The destruction issue is not only important in theory, but also relevant
 to reality.
 It can be used to explain a well-known puzzle of the happiness-income relations
hip in the economic and psychology literatures: happiness rises with income
 up to a point, but not beyond it.
 For example, well-being declined over the last quarter of a century in
 the U.S., and life satisfaction has run approximately flat across the same
 time in Britain.
 If we interpret income as a good, and if consumers envy each other in terms
 of consumption levels, but our result, when the income reaches a certain
 level, one may have to freely dispose of wealth to achieve Pareto efficient
 allocations; otherwise the resulting allocations will be Pareto inefficient.
 Therefore, economic growth does not raise well-being indeed by any social
 welfare function once the critical income level is achieved.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
11.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Production Externality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now show that allocation of resources may not be efficient also for the
 case of externality in production.
 To show this, consider a simple economy with two firms.
 Firm 1 produces an output 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 which will be sold in a competitive market.
 However, production of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 imposes an externality cost denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $e(x)$
\end_inset

 to firm 2, which is assumed to be convex and strictly increasing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 be the output produced by firm 2, which is sold in a competitive market.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{x}(x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{y}(y)$
\end_inset

 be the cost functions of firms 1 and 2 which are both convex and strictly
 increasing.
 The profits of the two firms:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\pi_{1} & = & p_{x}x-c_{x}(x)\\
\pi_{2} & = & p_{y}y-c_{y}(y)-e(x)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{x}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{y}$
\end_inset

 are the prices of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 Then, by the first-order conditions for a competitive equilibrium, we have
 positive amounts of outputs
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
p_{x} & = & c_{x}^{\prime}(x)\\
p_{y} & = & c_{y}^{\prime}(y)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

However, the profit maximizing output 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{c}$
\end_inset

 from the first order condition is too large from a social point of view.
 The first firm only takes account of the private cost, the cost that is
 imposed on itself, but it ignores the social cost, the private cost plus
 the cost it imposes on the other firm.
 What is the socially efficient output?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The social profit, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{1}+\pi_{2}$
\end_inset

, is not maximized at 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{c}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{c}$
\end_inset

, which satisfy (31) and (32), respectively.
 If the two firms merged so as to internalize the externality
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{x,y}}\ p_{x}x+p_{y}y-c_{x}(x)-e(x)-c_{y}(y)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which gives the first order conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p_{x} & = & c_{x}^{\prime}(x^{*})+e^{\prime}(x^{*})\\
p_{y} & = & c^{\prime}(y^{*})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is an efficient amount of output; it is characterized by price being equal
 to the social cost.
 Thus, production of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is less than the competitive output in the externality case by the convexity
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $e(x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{x}(x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
11.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Solutions to Externalities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the above discussion, we know that a competitive market in general
 may not result in Pareto efficient outcome, one needs to seek some other
 alternative mechanisms to solve the market failure problem.
 In this section, we now introduce some remedies to this market failure
 of externality such as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Pigovian taxes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Voluntary negotiation (Coase Approach)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compensatory tax/subsidy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Creating missing markets with property rights
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Direct intervention
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Merges
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Incentives mechanism design
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Any of the above solutions may result in Pareto efficient outcomes, but
 may leed to different income distributions.
 Also it is important to know what kind of information are required to implement
 a solution listed above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Most of the above proposed solutions need to make the following assumptions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The source and degree of the externality is identifiable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The recipients of the externality are identifiable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The causal relationship of the externality can be established objectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The cost of prevening (by different methods) an externality are perfectly
 known to everyone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The cost of implementing taxes and subsidies is negligible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The cost of voluntary negotiation is negligible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
11.4.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Pigovian Tax
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Set a tax rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, such that 
\begin_inset Formula $t=e^{\prime}(x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 This tax rate to firm 1 would internalize the externality
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi_{1}=p_{x}x-c_{x}(x)-t\cdot x
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The first-order conditions is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{x}=c^{\prime}(x)+t=c_{x}^{\prime}(x)+e^{\prime}(x^{*})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is the same as the one for social optimality.
 That is, when firm 1 faces the wrong price of its action, and a correction
 tax 
\begin_inset Formula $t=e^{\prime}(x^{*})$
\end_inset

 should be imposed that will lead to a social optimal outcome that is less
 than that of competitive equilibrium outcome.
 Such correction taxes are called Pigovian taxes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The problem with this solution is that it requires that the taxing authority
 knows the externality cost 
\begin_inset Formula $e(x)$
\end_inset

.
 But, how does the authority know the externality and how do they estimate
 the value of externality in real world? If the authority knows this information
, it might as well just tell the firm how much to produce in the first place.
 So, in most cases, it will not work well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
11.4.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Coase's Voluntary Negotiation and Enforceable Property Right Approach
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A different approach to the externality problem relies on the parties to
 negotiate a solution to the problem themselves.
 The greatest novelty of Nobel Laureate Ronald Coase's contribution was
 the systematic treatment of trade in property rights.
 To solve the externality problem, Coase in a famous 1960 article, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The Problem of Social Cost,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 argues that the success of such a system depends on making sure that property
 rights are clearly assigned.
 The so-called 
\emph on
Coases Theorem
\emph default
 assesses that as long as property rights are clearly assigned, the two
 parties will negotiate in such a way that the optimal level of the externality-
producing activity is implemented.
 As a policy implication, a government should simply rearrange property
 rights with appropriately designed property rights.
 The market then could take care of externalities without direct government
 intervention.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Coase Theorem thus contains two claims.
 One is that the level of the externality will be the same regardless of
 the assignment of propety rights, which is called the 
\emph on
Coase Neutrality Theorem
\emph default
.
 The second one is that voluntary negotiations over externalities will lead
 to a Pareto-optimal outcome, which is called the 
\emph on
Coase Efficiency Theorem
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Firms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Coase shows his propositions mainly by using examples of an economy with
 only two agents and a detrimental externality.
 Coase made an observation that in the presence of externalities, the victim
 has an incentive to pay the firm to stop the produciton if the victim can
 compensate the firm by paying 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{x}-c_{x}^{\prime}(x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 The following example captures Coase's arguments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
11.4.1.

\series default
 Two firms: One is a chemical factory that discharges chemicals into a river
 and the other is the fisherman.
 Suppose the river can produce a value of $50,000.
 If the chemicals pollute the river, the fish cannot be eaten.
 How does one solve the externality? Coase's method states that as long
 as the property rights of the river are clearly assigned, it results in
 efficient outcomes.
 That is, the government should give the ownership of the lake either to
 the chemical firm or to the fisherman, then it will yield an efficient
 output.
 To see this, assume that the cost of the filter is denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{f}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\emph on
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Case 1
\emph default
: The lake is given to the factory.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $c_{f}<\$50,000$
\end_inset

.
 The fisherman is willing to buy a filter for the factory.
 The fisherman will pay for the filter so that the chemical cannot pollute
 the lake.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $c_{f}>\$50,000$
\end_inset

.
 The chemical is discharged into the lake.
 The fisherman does not want to install any filter.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\emph on
Case 2
\emph default
: The lake is given to the fisherman, and the firm's net product revenue
 is greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{f}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $c_{f}<\$50,000$
\end_inset

.
 The factory buys the filter so that the chemical cannot pollute the lake.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $c_{f}>\$50,000$
\end_inset

.
 The firm pays $50,000 to the fisherman then the chemical is discharged
 into the lake.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Like the above example, Coase's own examples supporting his claims use negotiati
ons between firms and businesses rather than those between individuals.
 This difference is important since firms maximize profits rather than utility,
 and act as fiduciaries for individuals.
 We have to make some restricted assumptions on consumers' utility functions
 to make Coase's Theorem hold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Consumers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now consider an economy with two consumers with 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 goods.
 Further, consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has initial wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Each consumer has preferences over both the commodities he consumes and
 have some action 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 that is taken by consumer 1.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}(x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iL},h)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Activity 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is something that has no direct monetary cost for person 1.
 For example, 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is the quantity of loud music played by person 1.
 In order to play it, the consumer must purchase electricity, but electricity
 can be captured as one of the componenets of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 From the point of view of consumer 2, 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 represents an external effect of consumer 1's action.
 In the model, we assume that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial u_{2}}{\partial h}\neq0
\]

\end_inset

Thus the externality in this model lies in the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 affects consumer 2's utility, but it is not priced by the market.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}(p,w_{i},h)$
\end_inset

 be consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's indirect utility function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
v_{i}(p,w_{i},h)={\displaystyle \max_{x_{i}}}\ u_{i}(x_{i},h)\\
\mbox{s.t.}\ px_{i}\leq w_{i}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We assume that the preferences are quasi-linear with respect to some numeraire
 commodity.
 Thus, the consumer's indirect utility function takes the form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{i}(p,w_{i},h)=\phi_{i}(h)+w_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We further assume that utility is strictly concave in 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi^{\prime\prime}(h)<0$
\end_inset

.
 Again, the competitive equilibrium outcome, in general, is not Pareto optimal.
 In order to maximize utility, consumer 1 should choose 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 in order to maximize 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{1}$
\end_inset

 so that the interior solution satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{1}^{\prime}(h^{*})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Even though consumer 2's utility depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

, he cannot affect the choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One the other hand, the socially optimal level of 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 will maximize the sum of the consumers' utilities:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{h}}\ \phi_{1}(h)+\phi_{2}(h)
\]

\end_inset

The first-order condition for an interior maximum is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi_{1}^{\prime}(h^{**})+\phi_{2}^{\prime}(h^{**})=0
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{**}$
\end_inset

 is the Pareto optimal amount of 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the social optimum is where the sum of the marginal benefit of the
 two consumers equal zero.
 In the case where the externality is bad for consumer 2 (loud music), the
 level of 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{*}>h^{**}$
\end_inset

.
 That is, too much 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is produced.
 In the case where the externality is good for consumer 2 (yard beautification),
 too little will be provided, 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{*}<h^{**}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we show that as long as property rights are clearly assigned, the two
 parties will negotiate in such a way that the optimal level of the externality-
producing activity is implemented.
 We first consider the case where consumer 2 has the right to prohibit consumer
 1 from undertaking activity 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
 But, this right is contractible.
 Consumer 2 can sell consumer 1 the right to undertake 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{2}$
\end_inset

 units of activity 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 in exchange for some transfer, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The two consumers will bargain both over the size of the transfer 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{2}$
\end_inset

, and over the number of units of the externality good produced, 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to determine the outcome of the bargaining, we first specify the
 bargaining mechansim as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Consumer 2 offers consumer 1 a take-it-or-leave it contract specifying a
 payment 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{2}$
\end_inset

 and an activity level 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If consumer 1 accepts the offer, that outcome is implemented.
 If consumer 1 does not accept the offer, consumer 1 cannot produce any
 of the externality good, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $h=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To analyze this, begin by considering which offers 
\begin_inset Formula $(h,T)$
\end_inset

 will be accepted by consumer 1.
 Since in the absence of agreement, consumer 1 must produce 
\begin_inset Formula $h=0$
\end_inset

, consumer 1 will accept 
\begin_inset Formula $(h_{2},T_{2})$
\end_inset

 if and only if it offers higher utility than 
\begin_inset Formula $h=0$
\end_inset

.
 That is, consumer 1 accepts if and only if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi_{1}(h)-T_{2}\geq\phi_{1}(0)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Give this constraint on the set of acceptable offers, consumer 2 will choose
 
\begin_inset Formula $(h_{2},T_{2})$
\end_inset

 in order to solve the following problem:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{h,T}} & \phi_{2}(h)+T_{2}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & \phi_{1}(h)-T_{2}\geq\phi_{1}(0)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

 prefers higher 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, then the constraint will bind at the optimum.
 Thus, the problem becomes:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{h}}\ \phi_{1}(h)+\phi_{2}(h)-\phi_{1}(0)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first-order condition for this problem is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi_{1}^{\prime}(h_{2})+\phi_{2}^{\prime}(h_{2})=0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

But, this is the same condition that defines the socially optimal level
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, consumer 2 chooses 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{2}=h^{**}$
\end_inset

, and using the constraint, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{2}=\phi_{1}(h^{**})-\phi_{1}(0)$
\end_inset

.
 As a result, the offer 
\begin_inset Formula $(h_{2},T_{2})$
\end_inset

 is accepted by consumer 1, so this bargaining process implements the social
 optimum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we consider the case where consumer 1 has the right to produce as much
 externality as he wants.
 We maintain the same bargianing mechanism.
 Consumer 2 makes consumer 1 a take-it-or-leave-it offer 
\begin_inset Formula $(h_{1},T_{1})$
\end_inset

, where the subscript indicates that consumer 1 has the property right in
 this situation.
 However, now, in the event that 1 rejects the offer, consumer 1 can choose
 to produce as much of the externality as he wants, which means that he
 will choose to produce 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the only change between this situation and the first case is what
 happens in the event that no agreement is reached.
 In this case, consumer 2's problem is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{h,T}} & \phi_{2}(h)+T_{1}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & \phi_{1}(h)-T_{1}\geq\phi_{1}(h^{*})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Again, we know that the constraint will bind, and so consumer 2 chooses
 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}$
\end_inset

 in order to maximize
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max\ \phi_{1}(h)+\phi_{2}(h)-\phi_{1}(h^{*})
\]

\end_inset

which is also maximized at 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{1}=h^{**}$
\end_inset

, since the first-order condition is the same.
 The only differene is in the transfer.
 Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}=\phi(h^{**})-\phi_{1}(h^{*})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

While both property-rights allocations implement 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{**}$
\end_inset

, they have different distributional consequences.
 The transfer is larger in the case where consumer 2 has the property rights
 than when consumer 1 has the property rights.
 The reason for this is that consumer 2 is in a better bargaining position
 for the non-bargaining outcome when consumer 1 is forced to produce 0 units
 of the externality good.
 However, note that in the quasilinear framework, resdistribution of the
 numeraire commodity has no effect on social welfare.
 The fact that regardless of how the property rights are allocated, bargaining
 leads to a Pareto optimal allocation is an example of the Coase Theorem:
 If trade of the externality can occur, then bargaining will lead to an
 efficient outcome no matter how property rights are allocated (as long
 as they are clearly allocated).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that well-defined, enforceable property rights are essential for bargaining
 to work.
 If there is a dispute over who has the right to pollute (or not to pollute),
 then bargaining may not lead to efficiency.
 An additional requirment for efficiency is that the bargaining process
 itself is costless.
 Note that the government doesn't need to know about individual consumers
 here, it only needs to define property rights.
 However, it is critical that it does so clearly.
 Thus, the Coase Theorem provides an argument in favor of having clear laws
 and well-developed courts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Coase Neutrality Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\emph on
11.4.1 (Coase Neutrality Theorem).

\series default
\emph default
 In a pollution economy considered in the chapter, suppose that the transaction
 cost equals zero, and that the utility functions 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{i}(x_{i},h)$
\end_inset

 are differentiable and such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial U_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial U_{1}}{\partial h}>0$
\end_inset

 but 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial U_{2}}{\partial h}<0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},h)\in(0,\xi)\times(0,\eta),\ i=1,2$
\end_inset

.
 Then, the level of pollution is independent of the assignments of property
 rights if and only if the utility functions 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{i}(x,h)$
\end_inset

 up to a monotonic transformation, have a function form given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U_{i}(x_{i},h)=x_{i}\exp\left({\displaystyle \int}\psi(h)\right)+{\displaystyle \int}\exp\left({\displaystyle \int}\psi(h)\right)b_{i}(h)dh
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

 are arbitrary functions such that the 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{i}(x_{i},h)$
\end_inset

 are differentiable, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial U_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial U_{1}}{\partial h}>0$
\end_inset

 but 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial U_{2}}{\partial h}<0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},h)\in(0,\xi)\times(0,\eta),\ i=1,2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Although the above Coase Neutrality Theorem covers a wider class of preferences
 than quasi-linear utility functions, it still puts a significant restriciton
 on the domain of its validity due to the special functional forms of the
 utility functions with respect to the private good.
 Thus, the Coase Neutrality Theorem may be best applied to production externalit
ies between firms, rather than consumption externalities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Problems with the Coase Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The problem of the Coase Neutrality Theorem is that costs of negotiations
 and organization, in general, are not negligible, and the income effect
 may not be zero.
 Thus, a privatization is optimal only in cases of zero transaction costs,
 no income effect, and perfect economic environments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The problem of the Coase Efficiency Theorem is more serious.
 The base postulate underlying Coase's theory appears to be that the process
 of negotiation over property rights can be modelled as a cooperative game,
 and this requires the assumption that each play know the preferences or
 production functions of each of the other players.
 When information is not complete or asymmetric, in general, we do not have
 a Pareto optimal outcome.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For instance, when there is one polluter and there are many pollutees, a
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

free-rider
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 problem arises and there is an incentive for pollutees to misrepresent
 their preferences.
 Whether the polluter is liable or not, the pollutees may be expected to
 overstate the amount they require to compensate for the externality.
 Thus, we may need to design an incentive mechanism to solve the free-rider
 problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Secondly, even if the information is complete, there are several circumstances
 that have led a number of authors to question the conclusion in the Coase
 Efficiency Theorem:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The core may be empty, and hence no Pareto optimum exists.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
There may be a fundamental non-convexity that prevents a Pareto optimum
 from being supported by a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
When a agent possesses the right to pollute, there is a built-in incentive
 for extortion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the hypothesis that negotiations over externalities will mimic trades
 in a competitive equilibrium is as Coase himself conceded, not one that
 can be logically derived from his assumptions, but must be regarded as
 an empirical conjecture that may or may not be confirmed from the data.
 A lot of theoretical work therefore will remains in order to provide Coasian
 economics with the rigorous underpinning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
11.4.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Missing Market
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can regard externality as a lack of a market for an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

externality.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 For the previous example in Pigovian taxes, a missing market is a market
 for pollution.
 Adding a market for firm 2 to express its demand for pollution, or for
 a reduction of pollution, will provided a mechanism for efficient allocations.
 By adding this market, firm 1 can decide how much pollution it wants to
 sell, and firm 2 can decide how much pollution it wants to buy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 be the price of pollution.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the units of pollution that firm 1 wants to sell.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 is the units of pollution that firm 2 wants to buy.
 Normalize the output of firm 1 to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 The profit maximizaiton problems become
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\pi_{1} & = & p_{x}x_{1}+rx_{1}-c_{1}(x_{1})\\
\pi_{2} & = & p_{y}y-rx_{2}-e_{2}(x_{2})-c_{y}(y)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The first-order conditions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p_{x}+r & = & c_{1}^{\prime}(x_{1})\\
p_{y} & = & c_{y}^{\prime}(y)\\
-r & = & e^{\prime}(x_{2})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

At the market equilibrium, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}^{*}=x_{2}^{*}=x^{*}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{x}=c_{1}^{\prime}(x^{*})+e^{\prime}(x^{*})
\]

\end_inset

which results in a social optimal outcome.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
11.4.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Compensation Mechansim
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Pigovian taes were not adequate in general to solve externalities due
 to the information problem: the tax authority cannot know the cost imposed
 by the externality.
 How can onle sovle this incomplete information problem?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Varian proposed an incentive mechanism which encourages the firms to correctly
 reveal the costs they impose on the other.
 Here, we discuss the mechanism.
 In brief, a mechanism consists of a message space and an outcome function
 (rules of the game).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Strategy Space (Message Space): 
\begin_inset Formula $M=M_{1}\times M_{2}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{1}=\left\{ \left(t_{1},x_{1}\right)\right\} $
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}$
\end_inset

 is interpreted as a Pigovian tax proposed by firm 1 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the proposed level of output by firm 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{2}$
\end_inset

 is interpreted as a Pigovian tax proposed by firm 2 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 is the proposed level of output by firm 2.
 The mechanism has two stages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\emph on
Stage 1 (Announcement Stage)
\emph default
: Firms 1 and 2 name Pigovian tax rates, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i},\ i=1,2$
\end_inset

, which may or may not be the efficient level of such a tax rate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\emph on
State 2 (Choice Stage)
\emph default
: If firm 1 produces 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 units of pollution, firm 1 must pay 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{2}x$
\end_inset

 to firm 2.
 Thus, each firm takes the tax rate as given.
 Firm 2 receives 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}x$
\end_inset

 units as compensation.
 Each firm pays a penalty, 
\begin_inset Formula $(t_{1}-t_{2})^{2}$
\end_inset

, if they announce different tax rates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, the payoffs of two firms are:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\pi_{1}^{*} & = & {\displaystyle \max_{x}}\ p_{x}x-c_{x}(x)-t_{2}x-(t_{1}-t_{2})^{2}\\
\pi_{2}^{*} & = & {\displaystyle \max_{y}}\ p_{y}y-c_{y}(y)+t_{1}x-e(x)-(t_{1}-t_{2})^{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Because this is a two-stage game, we may use the subgame perfect equilibrium,
 i.e., an equilibrium in which each firm takes into account the repercussions
 of its first-stage choice on the outcomes in the second stage.
 As usual, we solve this game by looking at stage 2 first.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

At stage 2, firm 1 will choose 
\begin_inset Formula $x(t_{2})$
\end_inset

 to satisfy the first-order condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{x}-c_{x}^{\prime}(x)-t_{2}=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Note that, by the covexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{x}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{x}^{\prime\prime}(x)>0$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x^{\prime}(t_{2})=-\frac{1}{c^{\prime\prime}(x)}<0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Firm 2 will choose 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 to satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{y}=c_{y}^{\prime}(y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\emph on
Stage 1
\emph default
: Each firm will choose the tax rate 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{2}$
\end_inset

 to maximize their payoffs.
 For firm 1,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{t_{1}}}\ p_{x}x-c_{x}(x)-t_{2}x(t_{2})-(t_{1}-t_{2})^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which gives us the first-order condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
2(t_{1}-t_{2})=0
\]

\end_inset

so the optimal solution is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
t_{1}^{*}=t_{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For firm 2,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{t_{2}}}\ p_{y}y-c_{y}(y)+t_{1}x(t_{2})-e(x(t_{2}))-(t_{1}-t_{2})^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that the first-order condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t_{1}x_{2}^{\prime}(t_{2})-e^{\prime}(x(t_{2}))x^{\prime}(t_{2})+2(t_{1}-t_{2})=0
\]

\end_inset

and thus
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[t_{1}-e^{\prime}(x(t_{2}))\right]x^{\prime}(t_{2})+2(t_{1}-t_{2})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

By (38), (40), and (42), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
t^{*}=e^{\prime}(x(t^{*}))
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $t^{*}=t_{1}^{*}=t_{2}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Substituting the equilibrium tax rate 
\begin_inset Formula $t^{*}=e^{\prime}(x(t^{*}))$
\end_inset

 into (37) we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{x}=c_{x}^{\prime}(x^{*})+e^{\prime}(x^{*})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is the condition for social efficiency of produciton.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This mechansim works by setting opposing incentives for two agents.
 Firm 1 always has an incentive to match the announcement of firm 2.
 But consider firm 2's incentive.
 If firm 2 thinks that firm 1 will propose a large compensation rate 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}$
\end_inset

 for him, he wants firm 1 to be taxed as little as possible so that firm
 1 will produce as much as possible.
 On the other hand, if firm 2 thinks firm 1 will propose a small 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}$
\end_inset

, it wants firm 1 to be taxed as much as possible.
 Thus, the only point where firm 2 is indifferent about the level of production
 of firm 1 is where firm 2 is exactly compensated for the cost of the externalit
y.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In general, the individual's objective is different from the social goal.
 However, we may be able to construct an appropriate mechanism so that the
 individual's profit maximizing goal is consistent with the social goal
 such as efficient allocations.
\end_layout

